;
; I2S controller for the MVP chip: the bit clock, the serial data, and -- on the second
; engine -- the word select.
;
; The chip plays the *controller*: the device that generates SCK and WS (UM11732 §3, "the
; device generating SCK and WS is the controller").  One stereo frame is two slots, left
; then right, and a slot is one sample.
;
; Why two engines and not one
; ---------------------------
; A slot's word-select level must change exactly on the leading edge that is one whole
; clock period ahead of the edge that latches the MSB (UM11732 §3.1: "The transmitter
; always sends the MSB of the next word one clock period after the WS changes").  That
; edge is written by the *same instruction* that raises SCK, so on one engine the loop
; would need one copy of every instruction per channel: 2 instructions per bit cell times
; 2 copies times the slot length.  With 32 words of IMEM (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Engine
; state`) that caps a slot at 8 cells, i.e. 8-bit samples.
;
; Splitting the job removes the copy: engine 0 keeps one single HIGH/LOW pair and shifts
; the data out, engine 1 writes WS once per slot.  Both engines wrap on the same slot
; length and are started by the same CTRL write, so they stay in lockstep
; (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Clock divider`: "Engines restarted together stay in lockstep").
;
; The wire timing, from the spec
; ------------------------------
;   tick      ...   w        w + T/2        w + T  ...
;   bclk           _/¯\_/¯¯\_/¯¯¯\_            rising at w and w+T
;   ws             changes at w; next change at w + 16T
;   sd             the previous word's LSB until w + T/2, the MSB from w + T/2
;
; so a slot opens on a rising edge, the MSB's data cell is the half-period after it, and
; the LSB of a slot rides out on the *next* slot's opening edge (UM11732 §6.2 writes a slot
; out "On the next WS-level change").  Everything below is that picture:
;
;   * `nop side 1` is the HIGH half: it raises SCK through the side-set window and writes
;     no data, because SD must not move on the edge that latches it (`thtr >= 0` cannot be
;     resolved at zero hold, so the checker reads a change in the same tick as the edge as
;     a violation).
;   * `out pins, 1 side 0` is the LOW half: it lowers SCK and puts the next data bit on SD
;     through the OUT window, one half period before the edge that latches it.
;
; HALF_DELAY 3 gives a 50% duty cycle, 4 SM cycles per half and 8 per bit clock period.
; The rate itself comes from CLKDIV, exactly as on an RP2040: the program fixes the shape,
; the divider sets the speed (fw/i2s/driver.py, `### Speed`).
;
; Host configuration (docs/mvp-regmap.md `### Per-engine registers`), all of it built by
; fw/i2s/driver.py:
;
;   PINCTRL engine 0  SIDESET_BASE = OUT_BASE = the SCK and SD pins, SIDESET_COUNT = 1
;                     with EXECCTRL.SIDE_EN = 0 (one side-set *data* bit, no `opt`
;                     enable), OUT_COUNT = 1, SET_COUNT = 0.
;                     Both are 0..7 uio pins: only those can be driven
;                     (docs/mvp-isa.md `### The 18 protocol pins`).
;   PINCTRL engine 1  OUT_BASE = the WS pin, OUT_COUNT = 1, SIDESET_COUNT = 0 --
;                     the reset value is 2, and with SIDE_EN = 0 an engine that never says
;                     otherwise side-sets level 0 into two pins on every SM cycle.
;   SHIFTCTRL engine 0 OUT_SHIFTDIR = 0 (shift *left*, so the MSB leaves first, as
;                     UM11732 §3.1 wants), AUTOPULL = 1, PULL_THRESH = 16: one TX FIFO
;                     word per slot, and the host pushes it left-justified in bits 31:16.
;   SHIFTCTRL engine 1 OUT_SHIFTDIR = 1 (its reset value; the level is in bit 0),
;                     AUTOPULL = 0 -- engine 1 fetches with an explicit `pull block`,
;                     which is what stops the stream (see below).
;   EXECCTRL both     WRAP_BOTTOM = 0, WRAP_TOP = the last word of the program, so one
;                     wrap is exactly one slot.
;
; Stopping the stream
; -------------------
; Both engines run out of TX FIFO words at the same slot boundary and stall there:
; engine 0's next `out pins, 1` finds an empty OSR and an empty FIFO, and engine 1's
; `pull block` (its last instruction of the slot) stalls one cycle *before* the wrap.  So
; the final rising edge of the stream happens with no WS change, the last slot is
; complete, and the checker can close it: the stream ends where UM11732 §6.2 says a slot
; is written out.  Nothing here needs the host to know a cycle boundary.
;

.program i2s
.side_set 1

.define HALF_DELAY 3                ; a half period is 1 + 3 = 4 SM cycles

.wrap_target 0
.wrap 31

PUBLIC start:
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ;  0  HIGH half of cell 0.  This is the leading edge
                                        ;     that opens the slot: engine 1 writes WS here.
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ;  1  LOW half: SD gets the MSB of the sample
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ;  2  HIGH half: the MSB is latched here
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ;  3  SD gets bit 1
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ;  4
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ;  5  bit 2
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ;  6
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ;  7  bit 3
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ;  8
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ;  9  bit 4
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 10
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 11  bit 5
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 12
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 13  bit 6
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 14
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 15  bit 7
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 16
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 17  bit 8
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 18
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 19  bit 9
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 20
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 21  bit 10
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 22
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 23  bit 11
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 24
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 25  bit 12
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 26
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 27  bit 13
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 28
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 29  bit 14, the LSB.  It is latched on the next
                                        ;     slot's opening edge, one period from here.
        nop      side 1 [HALF_DELAY]   ; 30  HIGH half of the slot's last cell
        out pins, 1 side 0 [HALF_DELAY] ; 31  its LSB goes out on SD; the wrap then reopens
                                        ;     instruction 0, whose rising edge latches it
                                        ;     and, on engine 1, changes WS.

;
; Engine 1: one WS write per slot, then padding, and the fetch of the next slot's level in
; the slot's last SM cycle.  The slot is 4 + 4 + ... = 128 SM cycles long, which is exactly
; the 32 instructions x 4 SM cycles of `i2s` above -- the two wraps must be the same length
; or the WS edges would drift away from the clock edges (the checker's WS-EDGE rule).
;
; The `out pins, 1` is the *first* instruction, so WS moves on the slot's opening edge; the
; `pull block` is the *last*, so the level for the next slot is in the OSR before that
; opening edge.  Host-forcing one `pull block` before the engine starts primes the OSR with
; the first level, which is what lets slot 0 write WS in its first SM cycle.
;
.program i2s_ws
.side_set 0                          ; no side-set: this engine writes one pin and nothing else

.wrap_target 0
.wrap 4

PUBLIC start:
        out pins, 1              ; SM cycle 0 of the slot: WS for this slot, bit 0 of the
                                   ; word the previous slot's pull fetched
        nop [31]                  ; 32
        nop [31]                  ; 32
        nop [31]                  ; 32
        pull block [30]           ; SM cycle 127: fetch the level for the next slot.  With an
                                   ; empty TX FIFO this blocks *here*, one cycle before the
                                   ; wrap, so the next opening edge happens with no WS change
                                   ; and the last slot stays complete (see `Stopping the
                                   ; stream` above).